WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.084
The guarantees:
what each operation promises its caller,

2
00:00:04.084 --> 00:00:06.644
and what remains true when a silo stops.

3
00:00:06.994 --> 00:00:09.682
When Lattice fits named its limits.

4
00:00:09.682 --> 00:00:13.523
Here, we make the caller-visible
promises precise.

5
00:00:14.523 --> 00:00:17.721
The consistency guide classifies
the operations it lists.

6
00:00:17.721 --> 00:00:20.976
A method it does not list is
not classified by that table.

7
00:00:21.326 --> 00:00:27.102
Linearizable means a call takes effect
at one point between its invocation

8
00:00:27.102 --> 00:00:27.961
and return.

9
00:00:28.311 --> 00:00:32.721
Strong consistency means a result
reflects a real point during the call,

10
00:00:32.721 --> 00:00:37.437
without missing, double-counted or
misattributed entries as the tree changes.

11
00:00:37.787 --> 00:00:40.135
An online snapshot is correct per key,

12
00:00:40.135 --> 00:00:43.040
but is not guaranteed
to be one global instant.

13
00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.001
Eventual consistency
allows bounded staleness,

14
00:00:47.001 --> 00:00:49.961
then converges within
a configurable interval.

15
00:00:50.961 --> 00:00:55.569
With the default cache TTL of zero,
`GetAsync` is linearizable.

16
00:00:55.569 --> 00:00:59.373
A positive cache TTL makes
it eventually consistent,

17
00:00:59.373 --> 00:01:03.104
with staleness bounded
by the TTL plus one refresh.

18
00:01:03.454 --> 00:01:05.218
`SetAsync` is linearizable.

19
00:01:05.218 --> 00:01:08.483
Its write-ahead log append
is the commit boundary,

20
00:01:08.483 --> 00:01:11.096
before the in-memory projection changes.

21
00:01:12.096 --> 00:01:15.383
`GetManyAsync` is consistent per key
by default,

22
00:01:15.383 --> 00:01:18.739
but it is not one snapshot
across unrelated keys.

23
00:01:19.089 --> 00:01:22.988
`SetManyAsync` is linearizable per key,
not atomic as a batch.

24
00:01:22.988 --> 00:01:27.326
On failure, some keys may have committed;
sibling writes may still be

25
00:01:27.326 --> 00:01:31.099
in flight after the exception,
so re-read after they settle.

26
00:01:31.449 --> 00:01:37.243
`SetManyAtomicAsync` is all or nothing,
and atomic-visible across the tree.

27
00:01:37.243 --> 00:01:40.874
A concurrent reader sees every change,
or none.

28
00:01:41.874 --> 00:01:45.785
`ScanKeysAsync`
and `ScanEntriesAsync` stay ordered,

29
00:01:45.785 --> 00:01:49.695
without gaps
or duplicates through topology changes.

30
00:01:50.045 --> 00:01:53.865
Topology retries default to three;
resilient scans separately

31
00:01:53.865 --> 00:01:58.185
reconnect up to eight times,
with a smaller budget for stalled pages.

32
00:01:58.535 --> 00:02:02.337
Exhaustion faults rather
than returning an incomplete scan.

33
00:02:02.337 --> 00:02:06.074
A reconnect or resume captures
a fresh saga-decision view,

34
00:02:06.074 --> 00:02:11.293
so an atomic saga crossing that boundary
can appear pre-commit in earlier results

35
00:02:11.293 --> 00:02:13.226
and post-commit in later ones.

36
00:02:14.226 --> 00:02:17.439
An online snapshot is
not a strict snapshot.

37
00:02:17.439 --> 00:02:21.748
A snapshot cursor freezes
each shard's baseline during open

38
00:02:21.748 --> 00:02:23.647
and uses it on every page.

39
00:02:23.647 --> 00:02:28.613
The saga-decision gate keeps an atomic
batch on one side of capture.

40
00:02:28.613 --> 00:02:34.675
For a multi-page cursor, its baselines are
durable before the continuation returns,

41
00:02:34.675 --> 00:02:36.793
so it survives silo failover;

42
00:02:36.793 --> 00:02:41.905
eviction before the first read can
expire it, and it must be reopened.

43
00:02:42.255 --> 00:02:44.521
The WAL append is the commit point,

44
00:02:44.521 --> 00:02:48.083
but the provider decides
whether it survives a restart.

45
00:02:48.083 --> 00:02:52.292
The default in-memory log does not;
a durable provider is needed.

46
00:02:52.642 --> 00:02:56.220
If the silo hosting an atomic
saga crashes mid-flight,

47
00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:58.937
its keepalive resumes it on reactivation.

48
00:02:58.937 --> 00:03:00.992
A recorded commit is completed;

49
00:03:00.992 --> 00:03:06.293
an undecided saga is compensated once
its caller's decision deadline has passed.

50
00:03:07.293 --> 00:03:09.400
Next on Evaluate: The evidence.

51
00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:13.613
The deep dive on how this works is Clocks
and version vectors.

52
00:03:13.613 --> 00:03:17.554
The links are on this episode's page
in the documentation.
